  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You already know 1D DP store results in a single array, build answers from previous values. 2D DP is the exact same idea, just extended to&nbsp;<strong>two dimensions</strong>.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-what-changes-going-from-1d-to-2d",
      "title": "1. What Changes Going from 1D to 2D?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>In 1D DP:</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197058-262700008.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<code>dp[i]</code>&nbsp;represents the answer to the subproblem at position i.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each state&nbsp;<code>dp[i]</code>&nbsp;is computed using previous results like&nbsp;<code>dp[i-1]</code>,&nbsp;<code>dp[i-2]</code>, depending on the problem.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So you build the solution step by step using already solved smaller subproblems.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example:<br><code>dp → [0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8]</code>&nbsp;shows how each value is formed from the previous ones.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>In 2D DP:</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197058-598375882.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<code>dp[i][j]</code>&nbsp;represents the answer to the subproblem at row i and column j.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each state depends on nearby previous states like&nbsp;<code>dp[i-1][j]</code>&nbsp;(top),&nbsp;<code>dp[i][j-1]</code>&nbsp;(left), and sometimes&nbsp;<code>dp[i-1][j-1]</code>&nbsp;(diagonal).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So you build the solution cell by cell, using already computed values from above, left, or diagonal.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "dp →  [ 0,  1,  2,  3 ]\n      [ 1,  2,  3,  4 ]\n      [ 2,  3,  4,  5 ]"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each cell is filled using values from its neighbors, gradually building the final answer.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-when-do-you-need-2d-dp",
      "title": "2. When Do You Need 2D DP?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Whenever the problem involves&nbsp;<strong>two changing variables</strong>&nbsp;at the same time:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197058-281273433.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197058-281273433.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197058-281273433.png",
          "alt": "When Do You Need 2D DP?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If one changing variable → 1D DP. If two changing variables → 2D DP.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-the-classic-starting-point-nbsp-unique-paths",
      "title": "3. The Classic Starting Point &nbsp;(Unique Paths)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You are on a grid of size&nbsp;<code>m × n</code>. You start at the top-left corner. You want to reach the bottom-right corner. You can only move&nbsp;<strong>right or down</strong>&nbsp;at any step.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "How many unique paths exist?",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197059-870410807.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Thinking Through It",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To reach any cell&nbsp;<code>(i, j)</code>, you could only have come from:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To reach cell&nbsp;<code>(i, j)</code>, you can only come from two directions:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "From above&nbsp;<code>(i-1, j)</code>&nbsp;by moving down, or from left&nbsp;<code>(i, j-1)</code>&nbsp;by moving right.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So the total number of ways to reach&nbsp;<code>(i, j)</code>&nbsp;is the sum of ways to reach those two cells.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<code>dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This works because every path to&nbsp;<code>(i, j)</code>&nbsp;must pass through either the top cell or the left cell.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Base Cases",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197059-372043108.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197059-372043108.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197059-372043108.png",
          "alt": "Base Cases"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The first row can only be reached by continuously moving right, so every cell in the first row has exactly one way to reach it.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The first column can only be reached by continuously moving down, so every cell in the first column also has exactly one way to reach it.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These act as base cases, allowing us to fill the rest of the grid using the relation&nbsp;<code>dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1]</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "Filling the Table",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197059-460035915.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Start with a 3×3 grid and fill the base cases. The first row is all 1s because you can only move right, and the first column is all 1s because you can only move down.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-952808124.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Now move to cell (1,1). You can reach it from above (0,1) or from left (1,0), so add those values: 1 + 1 = 2.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-733001380.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Next, fill cell (1,2). It comes from (0,2) and (1,1), so 1 + 2 = 3.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-796038689.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Then fill cell (2,1). It comes from (1,1) and (2,0), so 2 + 1 = 3.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-159167570.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Finally, fill cell (2,2). It comes from (1,2) and (2,1), so 3 + 3 = 6.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At the end, the bottom-right cell gives the answer, which is 6 unique paths.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {\n    int[][] dp = new int[m][n];\n\n    // base cases — first row and first column all = 1\n    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) dp[i][0] = 1;\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) dp[0][j] = 1;\n\n    // fill the rest\n    for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) {\n            dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1];\n        }\n    }\n\n    return dp[m-1][n-1];\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Time complexity is&nbsp;<strong>O(m × n)</strong>&nbsp;because you fill each cell in the grid once, and there are m rows and n columns.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Space complexity is&nbsp;<strong>O(m × n)</strong>&nbsp;because you store the entire DP table of size m × n.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-how-to-approach-any-2d-dp-problem",
      "title": "4. How to Approach Any 2D DP Problem",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 1: Define the state clearly</strong><br>Decide what&nbsp;<code>dp[i][j]</code>&nbsp;represents, like cost to reach cell&nbsp;<code>(i, j)</code>&nbsp;or answer using first i and j elements.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 2: Find the relation (transition)</strong><br>Figure out how the current cell depends on neighbors like top, left, or diagonal.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 3: Set base cases</strong><br>Initialize first row, first column, or empty cases so computation can start.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 4: Fill the table systematically</strong><br>Traverse in an order where dependencies are already computed (usually top → bottom, left → right).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 5: Return the final answer</strong><br>Typically the last cell like&nbsp;<code>dp[m-1][n-1]</code>&nbsp;or&nbsp;<code>dp[m][n]</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-space-optimization-in-2d-dp",
      "title": "4. Space Optimization in 2D DP",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In many grid problems, each cell depends only on the current row and the previous row.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So instead of storing the full&nbsp;<code>m × n</code>&nbsp;table, you can keep just two arrays:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "one for the previous row and one for the current row.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-856533077.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "After computing a row, update&nbsp;<code>prev = curr</code>&nbsp;and reuse the array for the next row.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-891659032.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This reduces space from&nbsp;<strong>O(m × n)</strong>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<strong>O(n)</strong>&nbsp;while keeping the same logic.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tags": []
}
